        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base sm:text-lg text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
          Smooth acceleration, low day-to-day energy costs, and no worries about long-term battery resale value—here's what to know before leasing an EV.
        </p>

        <hr class="my-8 border-slate-100">

        <div class="space-y-6 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base">
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Why leasing pairs naturally with EVs</h2>
          <p>
            Battery technology and range improve quickly, which makes leasing a popular way to drive a newer EV without committing to owning it forever. At the end of your lease, you hand the car back rather than worrying about how future battery tech affects used values.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Charging and range tips</h2>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Home charging is cheapest:</strong> If you have off-street parking and a home charger, waking up to a full battery every morning—especially on an EV energy tariff—keeps running costs low.</li>
            <li><strong>Real-world range vs. weather:</strong> Cold winter temperatures and high motorway speeds reduce real-world range compared to official figures, so leave a comfortable buffer for regular long trips.</li>
            <li><strong>Public charging:</strong> Public rapid chargers are great for occasional long journeys, though they usually cost more per kWh than charging at home.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

